WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.818 --> 00:00:13.935
- [Mark] All right.

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:14.768
Geraldine.

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:15.601
- Yes

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:16.882
- [Mark] Geraldine. Where'd you grow up?

00:00:16.882 --> 00:00:18.692
Where are you from originally?

00:00:18.692 --> 00:00:19.525
- Here in the mountains.

00:00:19.525 --> 00:00:21.306
Leslie County.

00:00:21.306 --> 00:00:22.508
- [Mark] And tell me about your childhood.

00:00:22.508 --> 00:00:23.698
You had both your parents growing up?

00:00:23.698 --> 00:00:24.531
- Yes.

00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:27.959
- [Mark] How would you
describe your childhood?

00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:31.651
- Well, when I was about five-year old,

00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:34.360
I started goin' out to the mountains

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.193
pullin' wood out.

00:00:35.193 --> 00:00:36.129
Break it up.

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:39.629
My mom cooked on the wood stove, you know?

00:00:41.605 --> 00:00:44.989
My job was to help get
wood out and help cook

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:46.307
and clean house.

00:00:46.307 --> 00:00:47.466
- [Mark] What year was this?

00:00:47.466 --> 00:00:48.507
You were born in what year?

00:00:48.507 --> 00:00:50.138
- '49.

00:00:50.138 --> 00:00:53.150
I was about six, seven
year old when I started in.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.628
- [Mark] You had electricity

00:00:54.628 --> 00:00:55.728
and running water back then?

00:00:55.728 --> 00:00:59.041
- Oh no. We had to fetch
our water. No juice.

00:00:59.041 --> 00:01:02.046
We'd take our clothes to
the river and wash out

00:01:02.046 --> 00:01:03.242
on the rubbing board.

00:01:03.242 --> 00:01:04.075
- [Mark] Really?

00:01:04.075 --> 00:01:05.492
- Yes.

00:01:05.492 --> 00:01:06.952
- [Mark] How'd you get around you?

00:01:06.952 --> 00:01:08.813
You guys had cars back then?

00:01:08.813 --> 00:01:10.210
- We had a horse.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.822
We had a horse

00:01:11.822 --> 00:01:12.655
and sled.

00:01:15.451 --> 00:01:19.102
I travel through the
mountains from Bowen's Creek

00:01:19.102 --> 00:01:22.361
into Middle Fork through the mountain,

00:01:22.361 --> 00:01:26.694
and buy groceries and go
back home into Lower Fork.

00:01:27.758 --> 00:01:28.850
- [Mark] On horseback?

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:29.774
- On horseback.

00:01:29.774 --> 00:01:30.666
Yes.

00:01:30.666 --> 00:01:32.770
- [Mark] What did your
father do for a living?

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:36.570
- He worked in the
sawmill. A dollar a day.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.441
When I was little.

00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:41.091
- [Mark] Your father made a dollar a day?

00:01:41.091 --> 00:01:42.307
Working in the sawmill.

00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:43.140
- Yes.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.223
- [Mark] Wow.

00:01:45.651 --> 00:01:48.589
- I had two brothers older than I was.

00:01:48.589 --> 00:01:52.750
We had worked in the
fields, planting the garden,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.250
plowing the horse.

00:01:56.414 --> 00:01:59.223
And raising the garden
and canning and putting it

00:01:59.223 --> 00:02:01.423
away till the summer.

00:02:01.423 --> 00:02:04.549
And then winter time we
had to pull all the wood in

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:06.382
for the winter months.

00:02:08.191 --> 00:02:09.692
- [Mark] And how would you
stay warm in the winter?

00:02:09.692 --> 00:02:10.702
You burned?

00:02:10.702 --> 00:02:13.503
- We burned wood and a little bit of coal

00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:15.773
when we dug that out of the mountains.

00:02:15.773 --> 00:02:18.835
There weren't no coal mining back then.

00:02:18.835 --> 00:02:20.752
- [Mark] Is that right?

00:02:21.828 --> 00:02:24.562
So the coal mining started when?

00:02:24.562 --> 00:02:28.062
- Well, it started it later on and all ...

00:02:35.673 --> 00:02:39.003
It started pretty well in 1960.

00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:40.842
- [Mark] Is that right?

00:02:40.842 --> 00:02:42.371
How far did you go in school?

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:43.631
I went to eighth grade.

00:02:43.631 --> 00:02:45.942
- [Mark] Eighth grade.

00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:47.172
And you have children of your own?

00:02:47.172 --> 00:02:48.502
- Yes.

00:02:48.502 --> 00:02:49.860
- [Mark] You got married?

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.403
- I got married and got a divorce.

00:02:52.403 --> 00:02:54.112
Raised five boys on my own.

00:02:54.112 --> 00:02:54.945
No help.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.958
- [Mark] What year did
you start having kids

00:02:57.958 --> 00:02:59.979
or get married?

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.400
How old were you?

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.811
- I was I almost 17 when I got married.

00:03:02.811 --> 00:03:03.811
- [Mark] 17.

00:03:05.333 --> 00:03:06.681
- Back when I was young,

00:03:06.681 --> 00:03:08.683
we all lived in a two room shack.

00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:12.350
You know, a shack house
had two rooms in it.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.539
Didn't have no insulation or
nothing like that, you know?

00:03:18.539 --> 00:03:22.090
And then came that big
flood in 1957 through here.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.551
It got way up in the house and we all had

00:03:24.551 --> 00:03:28.321
to leave home and go on down the road.

00:03:28.321 --> 00:03:31.753
Yeah, it come through the
house, but we had to leave.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:32.928
- [Mark] Right.

00:03:32.928 --> 00:03:34.349
We had hit the mountains.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:35.182
- [Mark] Is that right?

00:03:35.182 --> 00:03:36.015
- Yeah.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:37.260
- [Mark] Did it destroy your house?

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.102
- Well, yeah, the water was up knee deep.

00:03:40.102 --> 00:03:41.185
- [Mark] Wow.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:46.278
- Washed a lot of our
chickens away, our hogs

00:03:46.278 --> 00:03:47.111
and cows.

00:03:48.666 --> 00:03:52.249
We had milk cows we
had to go out and milk.

00:03:54.325 --> 00:03:55.158
And chickens.

00:03:55.158 --> 00:03:56.005
- [Mark] And that's what you did for food?

00:03:56.005 --> 00:03:57.758
You grew your own food mostly?

00:03:57.758 --> 00:03:59.389
- Yeah.

00:03:59.389 --> 00:04:00.536
- [Mark] And how many kids do you have?

00:04:00.536 --> 00:04:01.703
- I have five.

00:04:02.788 --> 00:04:05.556
One got killed in a car wreck in 2003.

00:04:05.556 --> 00:04:07.139
- [Mark] I'm sorry.

00:04:10.427 --> 00:04:13.427
What was the best part of your life?

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.124
- Well, I raised my boys up

00:04:17.124 --> 00:04:17.957
on my own,

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:18.790
and

00:04:21.318 --> 00:04:24.068
the last 20 years it's been fine.

00:04:25.711 --> 00:04:27.233
Well living.

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.096
- [Mark] Your life has been good?

00:04:29.096 --> 00:04:29.929
- Yeah. Pretty well.

00:04:29.929 --> 00:04:30.762
- [Mark] You're happy?

00:04:30.762 --> 00:04:32.236
- Yeah.

00:04:32.236 --> 00:04:34.599
- [Mark] Did you ever
consider leaving Appalachia?

00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:35.596
- No.

00:04:35.596 --> 00:04:36.429
- [Mark] Never?

00:04:36.429 --> 00:04:37.597
- No.

00:04:37.597 --> 00:04:40.450
- [Mark] What do you love most about it?

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:44.987
- I love most about it, I
told you, the mountains.

00:04:44.987 --> 00:04:47.640
I love to coon hunt and squirrel hunt.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.731
I ain't into no turkey hunting or deers.

00:04:50.731 --> 00:04:54.047
I love coon hunt and squirrel hunt.

00:04:54.047 --> 00:04:54.880
- [Mark] And what do you do with

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.386
raccoon and squirrel?

00:04:56.386 --> 00:04:57.439
- You eat it.

00:04:57.439 --> 00:04:58.707
- [Mark] You eat them?

00:04:58.707 --> 00:04:59.621
- Yeah.

00:04:59.621 --> 00:05:00.716
- [Mark] Still to this day?

00:05:00.716 --> 00:05:01.839
- Yup.

00:05:01.839 --> 00:05:03.429
- [Mark] What does raccoon taste like?

00:05:03.429 --> 00:05:07.096
- I didn't like them,
but I love squirrels.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:11.174
So I coon hunted, squirrel hunted.

00:05:14.131 --> 00:05:15.798
Ginseng root to eat,

00:05:18.625 --> 00:05:20.363
and yard sale a lot.

00:05:20.363 --> 00:05:21.196
Ya know?

00:05:27.614 --> 00:05:28.595
- [Mark] And your sons have stayed

00:05:28.595 --> 00:05:29.806
here in Appalachia?

00:05:29.806 --> 00:05:30.807
- Yes.

00:05:30.807 --> 00:05:33.754
- [Mark] And what do they do for work?

00:05:33.754 --> 00:05:38.173
- Well, they work in the coal
mine a little bit, Randy.

00:05:38.173 --> 00:05:41.247
Michael, he never did have no public job,

00:05:41.247 --> 00:05:45.103
but he had little odd
jobs, he draw a check.

00:05:45.103 --> 00:05:48.715
And my oldest son, he's
on check now. He disabled.

00:05:48.715 --> 00:05:51.598
He worked building houses.

00:05:51.598 --> 00:05:53.647
- [Mark] None of your
family considered leaving?

00:05:53.647 --> 00:05:56.299
One. My older boy, Terry.

00:05:56.299 --> 00:05:59.741
He lives in Richmond. He been down there.

00:05:59.741 --> 00:06:00.869
He's got his own place down there.

00:06:00.869 --> 00:06:02.451
- [Mark] So he got out?

00:06:02.451 --> 00:06:06.284
- Garage and stuff like
that. He's doing well.

00:06:08.193 --> 00:06:11.325
- [Mark] What's the roughest
thing about living here?

00:06:11.325 --> 00:06:14.575
- The roughest thing about living here?

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:19.393
Probably in the winter time.

00:06:19.393 --> 00:06:20.473
- [Mark] It's hard to get around?

00:06:20.473 --> 00:06:21.562
- Yep.

00:06:21.562 --> 00:06:25.802
But now the last three or
four years it's been okay.

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:28.093
- [Mark] But the winters,
you got a heater.

00:06:28.093 --> 00:06:29.593
How do you heat your house?

00:06:29.593 --> 00:06:33.073
- I got a heater and I got a coal wood.

00:06:33.073 --> 00:06:34.411
- [Mark] So you burn coal to keep it warm?

00:06:34.411 --> 00:06:35.244
- Yep.

00:06:36.902 --> 00:06:37.854
- [Mark] Have you ever traveled?

00:06:37.854 --> 00:06:38.774
You been to other states?

00:06:38.774 --> 00:06:40.191
- Yeah. I've been to other states.

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:42.942
I've been to Illinois and Indiana.

00:06:42.942 --> 00:06:44.942
Georgia one time and ...

00:06:47.481 --> 00:06:50.752
Tennessee. That's all. I don't know.

00:06:50.752 --> 00:06:53.112
- [Mark] But you choose to stay here?

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:53.945
- Yeah.

00:06:57.494 --> 00:06:59.973
- [Mark] What makes Appalachians different

00:06:59.973 --> 00:07:04.137
than people you see in
other parts of the country?

00:07:04.137 --> 00:07:05.220
- Well to me,

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:08.404
everybody's about life.

00:07:08.404 --> 00:07:11.172
They're all real friendly.
They will help you if

00:07:11.172 --> 00:07:12.005
you need help.

00:07:12.005 --> 00:07:13.352
- [Mark] They really will.

00:07:13.352 --> 00:07:15.352
And they watch over you.

00:07:18.095 --> 00:07:22.311
You know, your neighborhood,
they're real nice.

00:07:22.311 --> 00:07:23.404
And everybody is.

00:07:23.404 --> 00:07:25.237
They help one another.

00:07:27.399 --> 00:07:29.806
Everybody's the same life.

00:07:29.806 --> 00:07:32.459
It's the same. Every day doin'.

00:07:32.459 --> 00:07:33.875
- [Mark] It's just a shame
that there's no work here.

00:07:33.875 --> 00:07:37.686
- Yeah. We never did have
no work in Leslie County.

00:07:37.686 --> 00:07:38.836
Never did.

00:07:38.836 --> 00:07:40.087
- [Mark] You had coal mines, no?

00:07:40.087 --> 00:07:42.719
- Yeah. But they were further out.

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:43.840
- [Mark] Further out?

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.272
- Yeah. That was towards
Virginia, Harlan and Hazard.

00:07:48.272 --> 00:07:49.397
Places like that.

00:07:49.397 --> 00:07:50.647
- [Mark] Right.

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:56.680
- And some people, they
log in the mountains,

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.847
cuts logs down, you know, around here.

00:08:01.359 --> 00:08:03.843
- [Mark] Now this lifestyle,
almost seems to be

00:08:03.843 --> 00:08:07.472
left behind. Technology has
taken over most of the world.

00:08:07.472 --> 00:08:11.447
But here in Appalachia, it's still very

00:08:11.447 --> 00:08:12.280
simple.

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:13.113
- Yeah.

00:08:13.113 --> 00:08:14.048
- [Mark] A lot of people live

00:08:14.048 --> 00:08:15.952
without electricity,
without running water.

00:08:15.952 --> 00:08:19.539
And they don't choose to
leave. They love it here.

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:20.857
- Yeah.

00:08:20.857 --> 00:08:21.959
- [Mark] Do you think that'll continue?

00:08:21.959 --> 00:08:23.459
- Yeah. I sure do.

00:08:24.786 --> 00:08:27.859
Yeah. You know we live in a ...

00:08:27.859 --> 00:08:30.942
If you look at them other big cities,

00:08:31.906 --> 00:08:34.156
they got a lot of problems.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:39.249
There fighting going on,
tearing the city apart.

00:08:39.249 --> 00:08:41.998
All those things goin' on right now.

00:08:41.998 --> 00:08:45.081
California's burning down the people.

00:08:46.297 --> 00:08:49.130
One good thing about it. A
lot of people like to live

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.307
back here in them mountains.

00:08:51.307 --> 00:08:52.668
Get away from all that.

00:08:52.668 --> 00:08:53.507
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:08:53.507 --> 00:08:57.399
You can live a very isolated
life here if you want to.

00:08:57.399 --> 00:08:58.566
- Yeah. Uh-huh

00:09:00.742 --> 00:09:02.566
And most people that come
from Appalachia really

00:09:02.566 --> 00:09:03.889
like this place too.

00:09:03.889 --> 00:09:05.500
- [Mark] It's Beautiful.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.598
It's one of the most
beautiful places I've seen.

00:09:07.598 --> 00:09:09.427
- Yeah. That's what everybody says.

00:09:09.427 --> 00:09:12.344
- [Mark] Are you a religious woman?

00:09:14.196 --> 00:09:16.404
- I'm a holiness. Yeah.

00:09:16.404 --> 00:09:17.742
- [Mark] Do you go to church?

00:09:17.742 --> 00:09:19.992
- Yeah, when I get to I do.

00:09:22.012 --> 00:09:23.033
- [Mark] What's been the hardest thing

00:09:23.033 --> 00:09:25.891
you've had to go through in your life?

00:09:25.891 --> 00:09:30.304
- The hardest thing I went
through was giving my son up.

00:09:30.304 --> 00:09:33.654
When he got killed in that car wreck.

00:09:33.654 --> 00:09:37.421
That's about the hardest
thing I've faced so far.

00:09:37.421 --> 00:09:39.416
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:09:39.416 --> 00:09:40.791
- Me?

00:09:40.791 --> 00:09:42.582
- I got a birthday coming up in October.

00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:44.353
I'm gonna be 71.

00:09:44.353 --> 00:09:46.123
- [Mark] You're 71?

00:09:46.123 --> 00:09:47.104
- Yeah, I'll be 71 in October.

00:09:47.104 --> 00:09:48.187
- [Mark] Wow.

00:09:49.644 --> 00:09:52.633
Do think all the adversity
you have to endure here

00:09:52.633 --> 00:09:54.032
and get through ...

00:09:54.032 --> 00:09:56.520
It's just a struggle to
survive between making money

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:58.000
and getting around.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.450
And you know, every
house is a struggle just

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.667
to get through the driveway almost.

00:10:02.667 --> 00:10:05.480
Do you think that's what
makes Appellations so

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.865
helpful and so kind?

00:10:06.865 --> 00:10:09.614
- Yeah. Everybody lives
about the same way.

00:10:09.614 --> 00:10:10.555
- [Mark] Everybody has a struggle.

00:10:10.555 --> 00:10:14.335
- Yep. Everybody up in
here they draw a check,

00:10:14.335 --> 00:10:15.918
and on food stamps.

00:10:17.598 --> 00:10:19.265
There ain't no work.

00:10:20.666 --> 00:10:23.376
If they do work, they're probably teachers

00:10:23.376 --> 00:10:25.899
or something like that, you know?

00:10:25.899 --> 00:10:27.080
A mail truck.

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:28.878
There ain't much work going on here

00:10:28.878 --> 00:10:30.467
in this County for people.

00:10:30.467 --> 00:10:32.519
- [Mark] Yeah. And drugs
is kind of taken over too?

00:10:32.519 --> 00:10:33.537
- Oh, Yeah.

00:10:33.537 --> 00:10:36.186
- [Mark] Drugs have
been out of control here

00:10:36.186 --> 00:10:37.886
for like a couple of decades, right?

00:10:37.886 --> 00:10:39.206
- Oh. When they bought that crank in here

00:10:39.206 --> 00:10:40.706
that what done it.

00:10:44.962 --> 00:10:46.795
In this region anyway.

00:10:48.339 --> 00:10:51.104
They let that all come in here.

00:10:51.104 --> 00:10:54.784
And the people was raising
a little bit of pot

00:10:54.784 --> 00:10:56.716
out here in the mountains.

00:10:56.716 --> 00:11:00.486
They raised the pot to
support their family,

00:11:00.486 --> 00:11:03.576
and to buy their clothes
and shoes and stuff.

00:11:03.576 --> 00:11:07.378
They use it in the right way.
You know what I'm saying?

00:11:07.378 --> 00:11:10.961
Okay, they come in here and rat on people.

00:11:12.069 --> 00:11:16.097
And they would take you to
jail for it if they catch it.

00:11:16.097 --> 00:11:20.180
And also, how come there
not arrests for cocaine,

00:11:21.269 --> 00:11:22.102
heroine,

00:11:22.996 --> 00:11:23.829
and that

00:11:24.962 --> 00:11:25.797
meth

00:11:25.797 --> 00:11:26.880
and all that.

00:11:27.714 --> 00:11:30.296
And they don't do nothin' about that.

00:11:30.296 --> 00:11:33.265
And people are dying in piles
on that, but they still don't

00:11:33.265 --> 00:11:35.015
do nothin' about it.

00:11:37.393 --> 00:11:38.937
And I don't know why.

00:11:38.937 --> 00:11:40.916
They all know where it comes through.

00:11:40.916 --> 00:11:41.886
Deal with it.

00:11:41.886 --> 00:11:43.928
I don't know why.

00:11:43.928 --> 00:11:45.778
- [Mark] Why do you think so

00:11:45.778 --> 00:11:46.826
many people have drug abuse problems here?

00:11:46.826 --> 00:11:48.817
- Cause there probably ain't no work.

00:11:48.817 --> 00:11:49.859
Nothin' for them to do.

00:11:49.859 --> 00:11:51.033
- [Mark] Nothing to do.

00:11:51.033 --> 00:11:53.033
- That's what I believe.

00:11:54.465 --> 00:11:57.667
When I was married to my
husband, he'd make moonshine.

00:11:57.667 --> 00:12:00.798
I helped him a little
bit there off and on.

00:12:00.798 --> 00:12:02.965
And he made the moonshine,

00:12:04.904 --> 00:12:05.742
but the worst

00:12:05.742 --> 00:12:08.905
problem about it is when he made it

00:12:08.905 --> 00:12:10.827
he never was sober no more.

00:12:10.827 --> 00:12:12.195
He'd hit that bottle and kept getting

00:12:12.195 --> 00:12:14.612
wild, wild, wild, wild, wild.

00:12:15.767 --> 00:12:17.350
He drunk it all up.

00:12:20.335 --> 00:12:24.892
That made him fight like
a horse and cat. (laughs)

00:12:24.892 --> 00:12:27.225
Then it end up in a divorce.

00:12:31.208 --> 00:12:32.836
- [Mark] Do you feel like
life has gotten better

00:12:32.836 --> 00:12:35.412
or worse in the last 20, 30 years?

00:12:35.412 --> 00:12:37.184
- Oh it got better.

00:12:37.184 --> 00:12:38.328
- [Mark] it's gotten easier?

00:12:38.328 --> 00:12:39.904
- Yeah, easier.

00:12:39.904 --> 00:12:40.955
- [Mark] What were the biggest changes?

00:12:40.955 --> 00:12:44.314
Cause you grew up in a time
when there were no cars.

00:12:44.314 --> 00:12:46.135
There was no electricity, no water.

00:12:46.135 --> 00:12:48.604
And things have gotten easier now, right?

00:12:48.604 --> 00:12:51.968
Right? What was the biggest change?

00:12:51.968 --> 00:12:53.194
- Yeah, I got a car.

00:12:53.194 --> 00:12:56.586
I own a car about the last 20 years.

00:12:56.586 --> 00:12:59.866
I ran on most different vehicles.

00:12:59.866 --> 00:13:01.449
And I'll go places.

00:13:03.628 --> 00:13:08.594
Visit my sisters down in
the country and go places.

00:13:08.594 --> 00:13:10.483
I like to yard sale a lot.

00:13:10.483 --> 00:13:12.074
Love the yard sales.

00:13:12.074 --> 00:13:13.122
- [Mark] What kind of stuff do you grow?

00:13:13.122 --> 00:13:14.297
Do you have a garden?

00:13:14.297 --> 00:13:15.725
- Yeah, we grow a big garden every year.

00:13:15.725 --> 00:13:17.235
That's the way we live.

00:13:17.235 --> 00:13:18.456
- [Mark] What do you grow?

00:13:18.456 --> 00:13:20.413
- I can corn, beans and

00:13:20.413 --> 00:13:22.996
raise taters, and sweet taters,

00:13:25.004 --> 00:13:25.921
and pepper,

00:13:26.822 --> 00:13:29.155
and watermelon, mush melons.

00:13:30.144 --> 00:13:32.586
You name it and I had it.

00:13:32.586 --> 00:13:33.419
Cabbage.

00:13:34.523 --> 00:13:38.402
I can away four or five
hundred jars of food a year.

00:13:38.402 --> 00:13:39.235
- [Mark] Really?

00:13:39.235 --> 00:13:40.068
- Yeah.

00:13:43.563 --> 00:13:44.614
If it hadn't been for that,

00:13:44.614 --> 00:13:46.843
I wouldn't make it.

00:13:46.843 --> 00:13:50.625
Well, with my boys, honestly,
we got food stamps too.

00:13:50.625 --> 00:13:52.208
And I draw a check.

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:59.670
I got my disability and social
security outta my husband.

00:14:00.113 --> 00:14:02.471
- [Mark] Do you have any
regrets in your life?

00:14:02.471 --> 00:14:03.304
- No.

00:14:04.783 --> 00:14:06.972
Not that I know of. I don't right now.

00:14:06.972 --> 00:14:07.886
Yeah, I do. One.

00:14:07.886 --> 00:14:08.980
- [Mark] What's that.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:10.230
- Smoking them.

00:14:13.892 --> 00:14:15.700
- [Mark] Yeah. Those
will shorten your life.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:16.533
- Yeah.

00:14:18.972 --> 00:14:21.972
- Wish I had never seen one of them.

00:14:23.501 --> 00:14:24.604
- [Mark] All right, Geraldine.

00:14:24.604 --> 00:14:25.682
Well thank you so much
for talking with me.

00:14:25.682 --> 00:14:26.661
- [Geraldine] You're welcome.

00:14:26.661 --> 00:14:28.171
- [Mark] You're very interesting.

00:14:28.171 --> 00:14:29.004
Thank you.

